async: do not call back into receivers that already disconnected

When an Asyncable is destroyed on another thread than the one it
subscribed to a channel on, ChannelImpl::disconnect() disconnects it on
the Asyncable side at once, but only queues the removal of its receiver
record to the subscribing thread. If that thread never handles the
message (e.g. an audio worker during shutdown), the record keeps a
dangling Asyncable pointer, and ~ChannelImpl() -> clearReceivers() calls
async_disconnect() on the freed object, locking its freed std::mutex.

glibc and the SRWLOCK-based libc++ mutex usually get away with it; with
libstdc++ on winpthreads the lock fails with EINVAL, std::system_error is
thrown from a destructor and MuseScore aborts while tearing down the
global IoC container at exit (seen intermittently with MuseScore4 -o
x.mnx on UCRT64: 'terminate called after throwing an instance of
std::system_error, what(): Invalid argument').

The channel's IConnectable::asyncables set is exactly the set of
receivers still connected (Asyncable::async_disconnect() and
async_disconnectAll() remove themselves from it synchronously), so only
call back into receivers that are still in it.

diff --git a/src/framework/global/thirdparty/kors_async/async/internal/channelimpl.h b/src/framework/global/thirdparty/kors_async/async/internal/channelimpl.h
index b4f6821e..deee5108 100644
--- a/src/framework/global/thirdparty/kors_async/async/internal/channelimpl.h
+++ b/src/framework/global/thirdparty/kors_async/async/internal/channelimpl.h
@@ -213,7 +213,14 @@ private:
         {
             auto deleteAll = [](std::vector<Receiver*>& recs, Asyncable::IConnectable* conn) {
                 for (Receiver* r : recs) {
-                    if (r->receiver) {
+                    // Only call back into receivers that are still connected.
+                    // An Asyncable destroyed on another thread than the one it
+                    // subscribed on has already removed itself from
+                    // conn->asyncables, but its removal from this list is only
+                    // queued to that thread and may never have run; then
+                    // r->receiver dangles.
+                    if (r->receiver
+                        && conn->asyncables.count(r->receiver) > 0) {
                         r->receiver->async_disconnect(conn);
                     }
                     delete r;
